<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Croatia</h1><p class="st-meta">800 CE – 925 CE (approx.) · capital Nin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Early medieval Croatian duchy on the Dalmatian coast, ruled by the Trpimirović line, which became the Kingdom of Croatia under Tomislav.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> settled the Dalmatian hinterland in the 7th century. Around 800 a duchy emerged under Frankish overlordship, confirmed by the Peace of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-aachen">Aachen</a> of 812 between the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/franks">Franks</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borna-duke-of-croatia">Borna</a> is the first well-attested ruler.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Dukes ruled from seats at Nin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klis-fortress">Klis</a> and Bijaći, with a Latin-influenced Christian elite and Byzantine Dalmatian towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zadar">Zadar</a> and Split nearby. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trpimir-i">Trpimir I</a> issued a charter in 852 calling himself duke of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>. In 879 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/branimir-of-croatia">Branimir</a> won papal recognition, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> turned firmly to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Stone churches with interlace decoration and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscriptions survive.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 925 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomislav-of-croatia">Tomislav</a> united it with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-pannonian-croatia">Duchy of Pannonian Croatia</a> and is recognised as the first king, creating the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-croatia-925-1102">Kingdom of Croatia</a> (925-1102).</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-croatia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Croatia" width="320" height="264"><figcaption>Image: Hxseek (talk) 03:42, 15 January 2009 (UTC) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Balkans850.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 CE – 925 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Nin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Borna, Trpimir I, Branimir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Croatian; Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Croatia, Bosnia and Herzegovina</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Dalmatian Croatia, Littoral Croatia</dd></dl></aside></div>
